{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And Jacob separated the lambs.</b> Rashi explained (Rashi on Genesis 30:40): those born akudim and nekudim he set apart for themselves and made them into a separate flock, and he led that akud flock before the flocks, and the faces of the flocks walking behind them gazed at these. And this is what it says, \"and he set the faces of the flocks toward the akod\" — that the faces of the flocks were toward the akod and toward every hum (dark brown) found in Laban's flocks — \"and he made for himself flocks alone and did not put them with Laban's flocks,\" as I have explained. This is the language of the Rav. But his words here are not correct, for why did he separate the lambs so that no nakod or hum remained in Laban's flock, neither among the lambs nor among the goats? And if the separated ones were those that the flocks had given birth to as akudim, nekudim, and berudim that were his, and from them he made this akud flock, why did he separate the lambs alone and not also take the he-goats and the goats that were born to him likewise, and make from all of them this akud flock that he led before the flocks? Furthermore, it never says that hum was born there. Furthermore, according to the Rav's opinion there are among the lambs no akod or taluy that are not his wages, and he made no rods for them. Rather, the meaning of the verse is that he separated the lambs from the goats and made of them a distinct flock — so he had a flock of lambs and a flock of goats. And he set the faces of all the flocks, both the flock of lambs and the flock of goats, toward the akudim and toward every hum that was in all of Laban's flocks, meaning that he placed the akudim before the goats and every hum before the lambs according to the opinion of the earlier commentators, or the akudim and every hum before the lambs according to our opinion, for the need for separation was because of the hum, which was his wage among the lambs alone. And the correct interpretation in my eyes is to say that Jacob separated the lambs, and set the faces of the aforementioned flocks — which are the lambs — toward the akod and toward every hum, and he made for himself flocks alone from the akod and the hum and led them before the lambs, and did not put them with Laban's flocks because they were his wages. And the reason for \"Laban's flocks\" is that he did so with all of Laban's flocks. Now the akod among the lambs were his. And do not ask why it says \"the lambs\" and then says \"the faces of the flocks,\" for it is the way of Scriptures to speak thus, and in this very parashah in a similar case: \"on the mountain of Gilead\" (Genesis 31:21). And the reason he did so with the lambs is that there was no hum among the rods, or because he knew from the nature of lambs that they require many signs on account of their heaviness more than the he-goats on account of their lightness."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The phrase 'on account of their heaviness... on account of their lightness' refers to the respective temperament of ewes vs. he-goats in relation to visual stimulation at conception; Ramban's reasoning is left as translated."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:54.700948549Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}